{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/noisy-motif-review.md", "content": "# Noisy motif transformation review

For a sparse colored motif embedded in a noisy binary field, record the exact changed-cell mask for every demonstration before proposing a rule. Distinguish seed motif cells, random background coincidences, and newly transformed cells. Check all apparent translated motif placements and treat untransformed near-matches as negative evidence; do not infer template-copying merely because some scattered locations resemble the seed. Compare complete masks across demos, including preserved cells and dimensions.

After an incorrect submission, discard the complete mechanism rather than adjusting only a palette mapping, phase, or a few cells. Rebuild the candidate set from all demonstrations and choose only a materially distinct rule whose trigger, extent, and exceptions explain every observed change. If evidence remains underdetermined, be candid internally about that uncertainty and spend the remaining attempt on the most demo-consistent whole-grid prediction, not a sequence of cosmetic variants.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-checkpoint/references/noisy-motif-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/separator-object-reconciliation.md", "content": "# Separator-and-object demos: reconcile before applying

When demos contain a thin separator color (such as a full row/column of 2), a uniform background, and a small colored object, do not infer that the separator itself should change or that the object simply fills its row/column. Record cell-level differences between input and output: whether the separator is preserved, which background line changes color, and whether the object moves relative to the separator. A demo can encode a coupled positional operation—for example, moving the object across the separator while recoloring an adjacent background line. Apply the complete relationship, not just one salient changed line.

For each test, map coordinates and orientation from the demonstrated relationship rather than copying the demo's absolute row/column. If evidence permits multiple positional mappings with different test predictions, spend a demo request only if it can distinguish them. After incorrect feedback, explicitly discard the failed mapping and revisit all changed and preserved cells; do not repeat it or make an unsupported minimal variation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/separator-object-reconciliation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/inert-demo-structures.md", "content": "# Preserved motifs are evidence too

When a demo's output is identical to its input, treat that as a real constraint rather than a non-example. A salient colored cluster can be structurally irrelevant, a distractor, or insufficiently contextualized to trigger the task rule. Compare across demos for motifs that change versus motifs that remain untouched, then apply only the supported transformation to the test. Avoid transforming a test cluster merely because it resembles the changed motif in shape or colors; establish which feature actually distinguishes operative from inert structures. If demonstrations do not resolve that distinction, request a targeted demo only when its expected information is worth the cost.
