{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/marker-ray-continuation.md", "content": "# Extending marker patterns as rays

When sparse input markers appear to encode a geometric trace, use demonstrations to infer its direction, step size, and colors rather than adding a one-cell continuation by intuition. Compare input and output coordinates: identify the seed points, infer the repeated displacement vector(s), and check the output for each predicted point. If the demo shows two rays or parallel traces, model both. Apply only the demonstrated continuation, stopping at the grid boundary and leaving unrelated cells unchanged.

A failed submission rejects the complete hypothesis but does not identify the bad cell. Revisit the full demo evidence and distinguish structural alternatives (for example, a single adjacent extension versus a repeated diagonal ray) before spending another attempt or requesting more examples.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/marker-ray-continuation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/failed-expansion-guess-review.md", "content": "# Failed expansion-guess review

In a dimension-changing task, output size alone does not imply tiling, repeated rows, reflections, or rotations. A large output from a single demo is weak evidence for its generator, especially when another demo has a different input shape or a strikingly different content arrangement. Before applying an expansion to a test grid, account for the full mapping from input features to output dimensions and every output region/cell across all examples.

A failed submission rejects the entire construction. Do not respond by submitting another ordering, orientation, or repetition of the same unsupported construction. Identify which assumptions were never demonstrated, then request a demo only if competing explicit rules predict meaningfully different outputs on the test. For this session, both proposed 12x4 outputs assumed a fourfold vertically repeated arrangement, while demos only established unrelated-looking resize patterns (8x7 to 24x14; 1x10 to 3x20); neither supported the test's 4x2-to-12x4 mapping. This is a cautionary example, not a replacement rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/failed-expansion-guess-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md", "content": "# Reconcile heterogeneous or seemingly contradictory demos

With costly demos that vary in size, palette, and visual structure, compare each input/output pair at the exact changed-cell level before choosing an action. Record preserved cells/regions and unchanged pairs as constraints. Normalize semantic color roles only where the edits support the mapping; superficial resemblance to a known task or a remembered reference is not validation.

Test a proposed shared rule against every example, separating trigger, selected extent, and replacement mapping. If some pairs appear incompatible, state that uncertainty instead of ignoring inconvenient examples. After a rejected submission, reject the whole compound hypothesis and rebuild the evidence ledger; do not switch transformation families based solely on unrelated skill notes or make unsupported local edits. Spend another demo only when explicit surviving alternatives predict materially different test outputs and the information justifies its cost. Maintain the required single-action JSON protocol.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint/references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/failed-hypothesis-revision.md", "content": "# Revising hypotheses after incorrect submissions

A wrong grid rejects the entire submitted hypothesis, but binary feedback does not locate the error. Do not respond by shifting the same speculative recoloring boundary or trying arbitrary minor variants. First compare every demo input/output cellwise and list the precise changed coordinates, including isolated endpoint changes and preserved lines. Distinguish object-internal changes from continuation/tail changes, and test predictions against all examples.

Request another demo only when you can state which live candidate rules it could separate. Otherwise derive a materially different rule from the examples and apply it once, preserving all cells not selected by that rule. This discipline prevents a sequence of unsupported guesses from spending the attempt budget.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/failed-hypothesis-revision.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/repeated-failure-checkpoint.md", "content": "# Repeated failures in costly ARC instances

When several submissions remain incorrect after many demonstrations, stop making loosely motivated cell-level revisions. Binary feedback rejects the entire rule and gives no localization. Reconstruct the full evidence ledger: exact changed and preserved cells, object and panel boundaries, exceptions, and each candidate's predictions across every demo. Then select a materially different structural hypothesis (for example, an aligned panel relation, localized overlay, or directional propagation) only if it explains the demonstrations better. Validate the whole proposed grid against all examples before submission. If demos are exhausted, avoid repeating prior grids and choose the strongest evidence-grounded alternative.

This complements the general checkpoint: demos must be reconciled immediately, and every action remains exactly one protocol-valid JSON object.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/repeated-failure-checkpoint.md"}
